WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
When a broken gene scrambles a tissue, which cells actually need it?

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.440
Did each cell fail on its own, or because its neighbours failed?

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.300
If every cell lacks the gene, both causes look identical.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.040
To separate them, place mutant cells beside normal neighbours.

5
00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:26.283
That is genetic mosaic analysis: making one tissue of two genetic kinds.

6
00:00:27.743 --> 00:00:32.783
Inside an otherwise normal animal, you mutate one cell and permanently mark it.

7
00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:39.563
Every descendant inherits both changes, forming a clone: a visible mutant patch amid

8
00:00:39.563 --> 00:00:40.563
normal cells.

9
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:44.797
Now watch the border where mutant cells touch normal neighbours.

10
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:49.257
If a gene acts inside the cell, only the patch changes.

11
00:00:50.577 --> 00:00:55.577
If it signals between cells, normal neighbours can change too, or supply what the

12
00:00:55.577 --> 00:00:56.577
patch lacks.

13
00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.410
On a fly sheet, touching cells talk by contact, and each becomes neural or

14
00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.030
epidermis, the outer skin.

15
00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.710
Delta is the signal saying, "not you: do not turn neural."

16
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.130
Notch is the receptor that hears it.

17
00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.990
Patches lacking Notch cannot hear, so they turn neural.

18
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.850
Predict: does a patch lacking Delta turn neural?

19
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.840
Patches lacking Delta do not turn neural on their own.

20
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.760
Losing Delta removes only what a cell sends, not what it hears.

21
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.440
They still have Notch, so normal neighbours' Delta still tells them, "not you."

22
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.540
So a mosaic separates sending a signal from hearing it.

23
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.880
Two traps can mislead.

24
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.080
Pulse labels like EdU dilute as cells divide, so marks must be permanent.

25
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.560
Marking two neighbouring cells at once can mimic one clone.

26
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.040
So controls mark sparsely and count close pairs in short-term samples.

27
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.880
They also check the mark stays off without the inducer, its trigger.

28
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:10.923
Mosaics also unlocked how tissues coordinate.

29
00:02:12.263 --> 00:02:14.303
Fly wing hairs all point toward the tip.

30
00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:21.603
In 1987, Charles Vinson and Paul Adler made patches lacking frizzled in normal

31
00:02:21.603 --> 00:02:28.003
wings. Normal cells on the tip side grew misdirected hairs; those on the other side

32
00:02:28.003 --> 00:02:32.443
did not. So frizzled's effect reached beyond the mutant cells.

33
00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:39.507
Those Notch and Delta patches came from Pascal Heitzler and Pat Simpson in 1991.

34
00:02:40.887 --> 00:02:42.787
They also varied Notch activity.

35
00:02:44.107 --> 00:02:50.167
A normal cell beside cells with less Notch activity became epidermis; beside more, a

36
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:55.527
neural precursor. Fate followed how a cell's Notch activity compared with its

37
00:02:55.527 --> 00:02:56.527
neighbours'.

38
00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.407
Mosaics even reach single neurons.

39
00:03:00.907 --> 00:03:07.007
MARCM labels one or a few neurons in an unlabelled brain, making only those mutant.

40
00:03:08.427 --> 00:03:14.347
In 2000, Tzumin Lee, Liqun Luo and colleagues tested fly brain cells called gamma

41
00:03:14.347 --> 00:03:21.767
neurons. Each needed the steroid receptor EcR-B1 inside itself to remodel at

42
00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:26.827
metamorphosis. Normal surroundings pinpoint where a gene must act.
